        {
          "tipo": "gramatica",
          "min": 12,
          "titulo": "The subject pronouns and the present of «ser»",
          "explicacao": "STEP 1: «ser» is the verb for saying who or what someone is: your name, your profession, your nationality and your permanent qualities. Its present tense is irregular and must be learned by heart: eu sou, você é, ele/ela é, a gente é, nós somos, vocês são, eles/elas são. STEP 2: in Brazil «você» is the normal word for «you», with friends, colleagues, shop assistants and strangers. It takes the same verb form as «ele» and «ela»: «você é». In some regions of the South and the Northeast people say «tu», and in Portugal «tu» is common too, but you can use «você» everywhere in Brazil and be understood. STEP 3: in everyday speech «a gente» (literally «the people») replaces «nós». It looks plural but it takes the verb of «ele/ela»: «a gente é», not «a gente somos». «Nós somos» sounds more careful and is common in writing. STEP 4: unlike Spanish or Italian, Brazilian speakers usually keep the subject pronoun, because «você é», «ele é» and «ela é» all use the same form «é». So say «Eu sou Marina», «Ela é Ana». Both «Sou Marina» and «Eu sou Marina» are correct. STEP 5: a name is introduced with «ser», never with «chamar» in this lesson: «Eu sou Pedro». Remember that «estar» also means «to be»; you will meet it in lesson 10. Today we use only «ser».",
          "comparacao": "In English «to be» does every job: «I am a teacher», «I am tired», «I am in Rio». Portuguese splits it into two verbs, «ser» and «estar». Today you learn «ser», for identity and lasting traits. English also has one «you» for all; Portuguese has «você» (singular) and «vocês» (plural), and «a gente» is a very frequent way to say «we».",
